@@ -37,3 +37,10 @@ The backend exposes a read-only context inspection derived from the current mani
 The final-context explorer MUST treat `manifest.files` as the authoritative included-file list. It may search/filter/paginate that list for usability, but MUST NOT infer that a file is included merely because a parent directory is eligible. Excluded directories may represent an entire skipped subtree and therefore do not require enumerating every child that was never walked.
 
 Structured exclusion details are local audit/UI metadata and MUST be omitted from the planner prompt payload when the legacy compact exclusion summary already communicates the same decision, avoiding duplicate context cost.
+
+
+## Git working-tree status (v1.2.19)
+
+Every context build records a bounded Git porcelain status in `manifest.git_status`, in `.harness/context-git-status.txt` inside the canonical context ZIP, and in the safe textual planner context. The status is captured after Wharness has established the project-local Git boundary. It includes branch/status information plus changed/untracked paths that are eligible under the same path-level context rules; paths denied by mandatory exclusions, `.harnessignore`, `whaness.yml`, automatic pruning, or symlink policy are omitted so Git metadata cannot reintroduce excluded/secret paths.
+
+The Git-status payload participates in the context SHA-256 fingerprint. A staging/index-state change can therefore invalidate planner context reuse even when working-tree file bytes are unchanged. Git-status capture is bounded and failure is represented as unavailable metadata rather than aborting the entire project context build.
